<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yun Gwan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1111">1111</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yun Gwan was a Korean general and statesman of the Goryeo period. After defeats by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byeolmuban">Byeolmuban</a>, a special army, in 1104. In 1107 he led it into the north-east, defeated the Jurchen and built nine fortresses, but they proved hard to hold and were returned to the Jurchen in 1109.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yun-gwan.jpg" alt="Yun Gwan" width="320" height="589" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Yun_Kwan_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yun Kwan was born on 12 July 1040, a descendant of the official Yun Sindal, and passed the Goryeo civil service examination in the reign of King Munjong. In 1087 he became a Chulchusa and inspected Gwangju, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheongju">Cheongju</a> and Chungju. When Sukjong became king, Yun was sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> to announce the coronation, and in 1098 he went as an envoy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> court with the same news.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> tribes to the north had long sent tribute to the kings of Goryeo, but once united under the Wanyan clan they began to cross the border and finally invaded. Goryeo, after a century of peace, had no strong army; the troops King Sukjong sent out were defeated, and Yun persuaded the Jurchen leaders to withdraw. Concluding that Goryeo lacked good cavalry, he asked the king's leave to reorganise the army into a professional force with trained cavalry units.</p><p>The Pyŏlmuban he led against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> in 1107 numbered about 170 thousand men. The Jurchen were defeated and surrendered to him, and he built nine fortresses north-east of the border to mark the victory. King Yejong then ordered him to withdraw his troops, and Yun brought back 346 prisoners, 96 horses and about 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Court rivals had him dismissed in 1108 and worked to have the fortresses handed back to the Jurchen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Released from prison in 1110, he was offered his command again but refused and went home, dying of illness on 15 June 1111. After his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and founded the Jin dynasty, and Goryeo, cut off from trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and its other neighbours, grew isolated and weaker. The reach of his campaigns has been disputed for centuries: tradition places the nine fortresses in present-day Hamheung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, while some sources suggest he took parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. His tomb was lost until the 18th century, and its site beside a tomb of the Sim clan began a feud between the Yuns and the Sims that lasted 300 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yun Kwan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1107 <b>Yun Gwan</b> led a special force, the Byeolmuban, against the Jurchen and built nine forts in the northeast, but they were handed back in 1109.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Paju, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.83,126.82&amp;year=1101" aria-label="Paju on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7713 803 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yun Gwan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8163 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8163 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.83,126.82&amp;year=1101">What was here in 1101? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
